tings.", "Incumbent Shelley Moore Capito (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Shelley Moore Capito enters the general election as a heavy favorite in West Virginia, a state that leans Republican by more than twenty points. She won renomination easily in May and has built a dominant financial position, with over $4.3 million on hand compared to roughly $22,000 for Democratic nominee Rachel Fetty Anderson. A Constitution Party candidate, S. Marshall Wilson, is also on the ballot with minimal resources.\n\nAll ten expert raters place the race at Solid or Safe Republican, and prediction markets give Capito about a 98% chance of winning. No general-election polls are available, so the estimate rests on state fundamentals, incumbency, and money. The forecast projects a Republican margin in the high teens, with Anderson facing an uphill fight against a well-funded, established incumbent in a favorable state and environment for her party.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.008, "margin": -17.55}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0111, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0053, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0155, "margin": -17.5}]}